exports[`supi help, pinned > supi agent create --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi agent create --name <name> --capability <key>... --external-id prn_… [--concurrency <n>]

Creates an agent record and links it to the principal it is on the organization plane, in one
request: a rejected link leaves no agent behind. The link is what lets the agent's own org-plane
tokens (a station token) resolve as this agent, and its capabilities are chosen here — they are
never carried in a token.

A linked agent is minted no spa_ token; supi agent mint-token issues one when it needs a native
credential. --external-id is therefore required here, although the route accepts an agent
without one.

A person's act: an agent credential is refused (403), because linking an agent to a principal is
what makes an agent token resolve at all.

Flags:
  --name <name>               what the agent is called on the board. Required.
  --capability <key>          a capability it holds; repeat the flag or separate with commas.
                              Normalised as a stage's owner is, so Code Review becomes
                              code-review, and registered if the workspace has not seen it.
                              Required. Repeatable.
  --external-id prn_…         the principal this agent IS on the organization plane — prn_ and
                              20 lowercase hex characters, checked before anything is sent. A
                              principal is one agent: one already linked elsewhere is refused.
                              Required.
  --concurrency <n>           the most cards it may hold at once; a whole number of at least 1.
                              Default: 1.

Who may run it:
  a person: admin or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused
  A session or a person's organization-plane token; never an agent.

Calls:
  POST /v1/agents

Examples:
  supi agent create --name Coordinator --capability coordination --external-id prn_0123456789abcdef0123
  supi agent create --name Reviewer --capability code-review,code --external-id prn_89ab… --concurrency 2
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi agent mint-token --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi agent mint-token <agentId> --kind claim-run|run-only [--out FILE]

Issues a fresh spa_ token for an agent that already exists. It does not expire; it lasts until
it is revoked in Crew → Agents, and revoking is per token, so an agent with two keeps working on
the other.

The secret is shown **once** — only its hash is kept. Without --out the token alone goes to
stdout and everything else to stderr, so > file captures exactly the secret; with --out it is
written 0600 and printed nowhere. With --json, the object's token field is the only place it
appears, and it is absent when --out took it. Nothing logs it.

A person's act: an agent credential is refused (403) — an agent must never mint itself, or a
peer, a credential that would outlive one a person revoked.

Arguments:
  <agentId>             the agent (see supi agents)

Flags:
  --kind claim-run|run-only   claim-run: scopes claim and run, for whatever takes cards off the
                              board on the agent's behalf; run-only: scope run, which drives the
                              card the agent already holds and cannot claim another — the one to
                              hand to a harness that reports for itself over MCP. Required.
  --out FILE                  write the token to this file, mode 0600, and print it nowhere. An
                              existing file is replaced, and its mode tightened to 0600.
                              Default: the token alone on stdout.

Who may run it:
  a person: admin or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused
  A session or a person's organization-plane token; never an agent.

Calls:
  POST /v1/agents/:agentId/tokens

Examples:
  supi agent mint-token agt_c4d2… --kind claim-run --out ~/.config/agent/claim-run.token
  supi agent mint-token agt_c4d2… --kind run-only > run-only.token
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi agent queueing --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi agent queueing <agentId> [--owner <userId>|none] [--boards <id,id,…>|none] [--ceiling <n>]

Sets the three bounds on an agent that queues work of its own. At least one flag is required; a
flag left out is left unchanged, and clearing is always typed (none), never inferred from an
empty value. The dispatch grant itself is not set here — it arrives in the agent's token from
the fleet that issued it.

Arguments:
  <agentId>             the agent (see supi agents)

Flags:
  --owner <userId>|none       the person whose work the agent's own cards are; none clears it.
                              Default: unchanged.
  --boards <id,id,…>|none     the boards the agent may queue onto, comma-separated. none — which
                              is also every agent's starting state — means NO board, not every
                              board. Default: unchanged.
  --ceiling <n>               the most cards it may queue in an hour; a whole number of at least
                              1. Default: unchanged.

Who may run it:
  a person: admin or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused

Calls:
  PATCH /v1/agents/:agentId

Examples:
  supi agent queueing agt_c4d2… --owner usr_19a0… --boards brd_8f2c…,brd_93fa… --ceiling 6
  supi agent queueing agt_c4d2… --boards none
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi agents --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi agents

Every agent in the workspace with the capabilities it declares, its token ids (never a token)
and its queueing policy.

Who may run it:
  a person: viewer or above in the workspace
  an agent token: the read scope

Calls:
  GET /v1/agents

Examples:
  supi agents
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi approve --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi approve <boardId> <gateId> [--comment "why"] [--delivery manual|automatic]

Approves a generic gate. For an immutable publishing approval, --delivery manual|automatic reads
the gate and binds that exact frozen subject and digest to the chosen delivery mode.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <gateId>              the gate (see supi gates <boardId>)

Flags:
  --comment "why"             recorded with the decision and shown to whoever reads the card.
                              Default: no comment.
  --delivery manual|automatic for an immutable publishing approval, bind the frozen digest to
                              manual or automatic delivery. Default: plain approval for a
                              generic gate.

Who may run it:
  a person: member or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused

Calls:
  GET /v1/boards/:boardId/gates/:gateId
  POST /v1/boards/:boardId/gates/:gateId/resolve

Examples:
  supi approve brd_8f2c… gat_19c0… --comment "checked the diff"
  supi approve brd_8f2c… gat_19c0… --delivery manual --comment "I will post it"
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi archive --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi archive <boardId> <cardId>

Archives a card now. It leaves the board's lanes and appears under the web app's "show archived"
filter.

Archiving also ends whatever the card was waiting on a person for, the same way moving it does:
its pending approval gates and pending questions are cancelled (so they leave Needs you and supi
gates), and an active approval subject is invalidated. A cancelled gate stays cancelled.
Restoring the card does not reopen it: a card that was waiting is restored parked as blocked,
saying its review was cancelled when it was archived. Resume it to an earlier stage (supi
resume) or move it to put it back to work.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <cardId>              the card, as crd_… (see supi board <boardId>)

Who may run it:
  a person: member or above in the workspace
  an agent token: the plan scope

Calls:
  PATCH /v1/boards/:boardId/cards/:cardId

Examples:
  supi archive brd_8f2c… crd_41aa…
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi board --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi board <boardId>

One board: each stage in order with its owner, gate and limits, and the cards standing in it.
supi board <boardId> --json prints the snapshot in the shape create-board --stages and
set-stages accept, so a pipeline can be read, edited and put back.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)

Who may run it:
