`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi help capability define 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi capability define <id|key> --definition <text>

Sets a capability's definition. Named by key, it is normalised the way a stage's owner is, so
Code Review finds code-review; a key the registry has not seen yet is declared with the
definition, so the vocabulary can be defined before any stage or agent names it. Its key never
changes — stages and agents refer to it.

A person's act: an agent credential is refused (403). Declaring the vocabulary is the same class
of act as managing the agents that hold it.

Arguments:
  <id|key>              the capability, as cap_… or by its key (see supi capabilities)

Flags:
  --definition <text>         what holding this capability means — the description the registry
                              and Crew → Capabilities show. Required.

Who may run it:
  a person: admin or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused
  A session or a person's organization-plane token; never an agent. A key not yet registered is
    a POST /v1/capabilities instead of the PATCH; named by cap_… id, the GET is skipped.

Calls:
  GET /v1/capabilities
  PATCH /v1/capabilities/:id

Examples:
  supi capability define code-review --definition "Reads a diff and says what is wrong with it."
  supi capability define cap_7a1e… --definition "Plans work and queues it onto boards."
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi help card 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi card <boardId> <cardId>

One card: its fields, stage, labels, references and state.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <cardId>              the card, as crd_… (see supi board <boardId>)

Who may run it:
  a person: viewer or above in the workspace
  an agent token: the read scope

Calls:
  GET /v1/boards/:boardId/cards/:cardId

Examples:
  supi card brd_8f2c… crd_41aa…
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi help comment 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi comment <boardId> <cardId> <text>

Adds a comment to the card, attributed to whoever holds the credential — a person by their
account, an agent by its name. It appears in the card drawer straight away for anyone watching.

An agent working the card sees it: the newest comments travel with the card when an agent claims
it, and an agent mid-run re-reads the thread (superpipeline_list_comments) before it finishes
the stage. A comment does not interrupt a run that is already working, and it is not a gate
decision — use request-changes to send work back.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <cardId>              the card, as crd_… (see supi board <boardId>)
  <text>                the comment, as Markdown text of at most 8 KB; every remaining word is
                        part of it, or - to read it from stdin

Who may run it:
  a person: viewer or above in the workspace
  an agent token: the run scope
  An agent may comment only on a card its live run holds; on any other card it is refused (403),
    whatever its scopes.

Calls:
  POST /v1/boards/:boardId/cards/:cardId/comments

Examples:
  supi comment brd_8f2c… crd_41aa… Please also cover the expired-token path
  git diff --stat | supi comment brd_8f2c… crd_41aa… -
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi help comments 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi comments <boardId> <cardId>

The card's comment thread, oldest first: who wrote each one (a person or an agent), when, and
what it says. A deleted comment is shown as deleted, with its author and time but not its text.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <cardId>              the card, as crd_… (see supi board <boardId>)

Who may run it:
  a person: viewer or above in the workspace
  an agent token: the read scope

Calls:
  GET /v1/boards/:boardId/cards/:cardId/comments

Examples:
  supi comments brd_8f2c… crd_41aa…
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi help create-board 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi create-board <name> [--template <id>] [--stages <file|->]

Creates a board from a template or from a pipeline you supply. --template and --stages both name
the pipeline, so pass at most one. The board checks the stages itself; this only refuses a file
that is the wrong kind of thing.

Arguments:
  <name>                the board's name

Flags:
  --template <id>             start from a shipped template (see supi templates). Default:
                              simple.
  --stages <file|->           read the pipeline from a JSON file, or stdin for -: a non-empty
                              array of stages, or an object with a stages array (which is what
                              supi board <boardId> --json prints). Every stage needs a string
                              key. Default: the template's stages.

Who may run it:
  a person: admin or above in the workspace
  an agent token: the compose scope

Calls:
  POST /v1/boards

Examples:
  supi create-board 'Releases' --template software
  supi board brd_8f2c… --json | supi create-board 'Copy of releases' --stages -
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi help create-card 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi create-card <boardId> <title> [--spec <file|->] [--priority <n>] [--due YYYY-MM-DD]
    [--label <id>...]

Creates a card in the board's first stage. The card records the credential that queued it as its
grant — who asked for the work and on whose authority. An agent's card is also bounded by the
queueing policy supi agent queueing sets: which boards may receive it, whose work it is, and how
many cards an hour.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <title>               the card's title; every remaining word is part of it, so it needs no
                        quoting

Flags:
  --spec <file|->             the card's structured spec, as JSON from a file or stdin. Default:
                              no spec.
  --priority <n>              the card's priority. Default: 0.
  --due YYYY-MM-DD            the due date; must be a date in YYYY-MM-DD form (checked before
                              anything is sent). Default: no due date.
  --label <id>                a label id from supi label list; repeat for more than one. Applied
                              by a second request after the card exists. Default: no labels.
                              Repeatable.

Who may run it:
  a person: member or above in the workspace
  an agent token: the queue scope
  The PATCH that applies --label also needs plan on an agent token. An agent's card is bounded
    by its queueing policy and dispatch grant.

Calls:
  POST /v1/boards/:boardId/cards
  PATCH /v1/boards/:boardId/cards/:cardId

Examples:
  supi create-card brd_8f2c… Write the release notes for 0.0.9
  supi create-card brd_8f2c… Audit the token routes --spec spec.json --priority 2 --due 2026-11-01 --label lbl_sec
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi help delivery 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi delivery <boardId> <gateId> [--mode manual|automatic] [--live-url https://…]
    [--remind-at ISO timestamp]

Changes only delivery mechanics for an already approved immutable subject. Give exactly one of
--mode, --live-url, or --remind-at; switching modes does not require a fresh approval because
the digest stays unchanged.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <gateId>              the gate (see supi gates <boardId>)

Flags:
  --mode manual|automatic     switch delivery mode without changing the approved digest
  --live-url https://…        record the URL of a manually published post
  --remind-at ISO timestamp   if no live URL exists then, remind the person and offer automatic
                              delivery

Who may run it:
  a person: member or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused

Calls:
  POST /v1/boards/:boardId/gates/:gateId/delivery

Examples:
  supi delivery brd_8f2c… gat_19c0… --remind-at 2029-01-02T03:04:05Z
  supi delivery brd_8f2c… gat_19c0… --mode automatic
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi help edit-card 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi edit-card <boardId> <cardId> [--title <text>] [--spec <file|->] [--merge-spec <file|->]
    [--priority <n>] [--due YYYY-MM-DD|none]

Edits a card through the same PATCH the web app's card drawer sends, with the same authority.
Only the fields named change. Labels, owner and archiving have their own verbs and routes.

--merge-spec is a read-modify-write: it reads the card, merges into the spec it read, and writes
back with expectedUpdatedAt set to the card's updatedAt as read. If anything changed the card in
between — an edit in the drawer, a claim, a move — the server answers 409 and nothing is
written; run the command again to merge into the card as it now is. --spec replaces the spec
outright and sends no precondition.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <cardId>              the card, as crd_… (see supi board <boardId>)

Flags:
  --title <text>              the card's new title (quote it if it has spaces). Default:
                              unchanged.
  --spec <file|->             a new spec, as JSON from a file or stdin. It REPLACES the spec
                              whole — every key not in it is gone. Default: unchanged.
  --merge-spec <file|->       a JSON object whose top-level keys are merged into the existing
                              spec: a key given replaces that key (a nested object is replaced,
                              not merged into), a key set to null is removed, every other key is
                              kept. Cannot be combined with --spec. Default: unchanged.
  --priority <n>              the card's new priority. Default: unchanged.
  --due YYYY-MM-DD|none       the new due date, or none to clear it; a date must be a date in
                              YYYY-MM-DD form (checked before anything is sent). Default:
                              unchanged.

Who may run it:
  a person: member or above in the workspace
  an agent token: the plan scope
  With --merge-spec the card is read (the GET) before the PATCH, which needs read as well on an
    agent token.

Calls:
  PATCH /v1/boards/:boardId/cards/:cardId
  GET /v1/boards/:boardId/cards/:cardId

Examples:
  supi edit-card brd_8f2c… crd_41aa… --title "Write the 0.0.9 release notes" --priority 2
  supi edit-card brd_8f2c… crd_41aa… --merge-spec acceptance.json
  echo '{"notes": null}' | supi edit-card brd_8f2c… crd_41aa… --merge-spec -
  supi edit-card brd_8f2c… crd_41aa… --due none
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi help forge 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi forge [<host>|none]

Shows or sets the workspace's forge host. GitHub needs no configuration; a self-hosted Forgejo
does, and until it is set a link to one of its repositories is stored as a plain URL — with no
durable id for events to match.

Arguments:
  <host|none>           a Forgejo host to set, or none to clear it; omit to show the current one

Who may run it:
  a person: viewer or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused
  Showing needs viewer; setting or clearing needs admin.

Calls:
  GET /v1/tenant/forge
  PUT /v1/tenant/forge

Examples:
  supi forge
  supi forge git.example.com
  supi forge none
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi help gates 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi gates <boardId>

Every gate on the board that is open and waiting for a decision, with the card it holds. A gate
on an archived card is never listed: archiving cancels it.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)

Who may run it:
