exports[`supi help, pinned > supi reject --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi reject <boardId> <gateId> [--comment "why"]

Rejects the gate. The card stops in the rejected state where it stands — refused, not returned
for rework. Use request-changes to send it back.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <gateId>              the gate (see supi gates <boardId>)

Flags:
  --comment "why"             recorded with the decision and shown to whoever reads the card.
                              Default: no comment.

Who may run it:
  a person: member or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused

Calls:
  POST /v1/boards/:boardId/gates/:gateId/resolve

Examples:
  supi reject brd_8f2c… gat_19c0… --comment "out of scope"
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi request-changes --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi request-changes <boardId> <gateId> --comment "what to change"

Returns the card to the stage the gate names for rework, where it is claimable again. The
comment is required: it is merged into the handoff the next run reads, so a request with nothing
said would re-queue the work with no instruction.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <gateId>              the gate (see supi gates <boardId>)

Flags:
  --comment "what to change"  what to change. It becomes the rework instruction the next run
                              reads. Required.

Who may run it:
  a person: member or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused

Calls:
  POST /v1/boards/:boardId/gates/:gateId/resolve

Examples:
  supi request-changes brd_8f2c… gat_19c0… --comment "add a test for the expired-token path"
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi resume --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi resume <boardId> <cardId> --comment <text> [--stage <key>]

Resumes a card in input-required — blocked by its agent, refused twice at its stage's completion
check, stopped by the circuit breaker, or refused dispatch. The card returns to its stage (or
--stage, an earlier one) as submitted, its "needs a person" reason is cleared, and the stage
starts over: the failure count is reset and the automatic rework is owed again. Agents that can
claim the stage are told it is available.

The comment is stored twice: on the card's thread, and as feedback in the handoff the next claim
carries — the same place a reviewer's request-changes comment goes. Resuming re-queues the card,
so you become its queuer, as on a move.

Not for an open question (answer it on the card) or a pending review (use approve or
request-changes): both are refused with a pointer to the right action. To send a card forward,
use move.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <cardId>              the card, as crd_… (see supi board <boardId>)

Flags:
  --comment <text>            what changed, or what to do differently. Kept on the card's
                              comment thread and handed to the next agent as feedback. Required.
  --stage <key>               send it back to this earlier stage instead. Default: the card's
                              current stage.

Who may run it:
  a person: member or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused
  Resuming is the human half of a block: an agent token is refused (403) whatever its scopes.

Calls:
  POST /v1/boards/:boardId/cards/:cardId/resume

Examples:
  supi resume brd_8f2c… crd_41aa… --comment "Staging is back up; run the migration again."
  supi resume brd_8f2c… crd_41aa… --stage plan --comment "Re-plan this without the staging database."
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi schedule add --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi schedule add <boardId> --title <t> --rule <r> --tz <tz> [--stage <key>] [--priority <n>]
    [--overlap skip|allow]

Declares a schedule that creates a card on the board each time its rule fires.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)

Flags:
  --title <t>                 the title each card it creates carries. Required.
  --rule <r>                  the cadence: every <n> minutes|hours|days, daily at HH:MM, weekly
                              on <mon-sun> at HH:MM, or monthly on <1-28> at HH:MM. The server
                              checks the grammar and its refusal says what to type instead.
                              Required.
  --tz <tz>                   the IANA time zone the rule's times are in, such as Europe/London.
                              Required.
  --stage <key>               the stage each card is created in. Default: the board's first
                              stage.
  --priority <n>              each card's priority. Default: 0.
  --overlap skip|allow        skip: do not create a card while the previous one is still open;
                              allow: create it anyway. Default: skip.

Who may run it:
  a person: admin or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused

Calls:
  POST /v1/boards/:boardId/schedules

Examples:
  supi schedule add brd_8f2c… --title 'Weekly dependency audit' --rule 'weekly on mon at 09:00' --tz Europe/London
  supi schedule add brd_8f2c… --title 'Triage inbox' --rule 'every 4 hours' --tz UTC --overlap skip
",
}
`;

