exports[`supi help, pinned > supi set-stage --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi set-stage <boardId> <stageKey> [--instructions <file|->] [--clear-instructions]
    [--name <name>] [--gate none|approval] [--wip <n>|none] [--owner <capability>]
    [--completion <file|->] [--clear-completion] [--return-stage <stageKey>]
    [--clear-return-stage]

Changes the named fields of one stage and nothing else. At least one flag is required. A person
may set any field. An agent token with compose may set --instructions only — the other fields
are routing, and the server refuses them by name.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <stageKey>            the stage's key (see supi board <boardId>)

Flags:
  --instructions <file|->     the stage's runbook — prose handed to whoever claims a card here —
                              read from a file, or stdin for -. A file rather than a string
                              because shell quoting mangles paragraphs. Default: unchanged.
  --clear-instructions        remove the stage's instructions. Default: off.
  --name <name>               rename the stage. Default: unchanged.
  --gate none|approval        approval makes a card wait for a human decision before it leaves
                              the stage. Default: unchanged.
  --wip <n>|none              the most cards the stage may hold at once; none removes the limit.
                              Default: unchanged.
  --owner <capability>        the capability an agent must hold to claim cards here. Default:
                              unchanged.
  --completion <file|->       what a run must produce before the board believes it finished, as
                              JSON from a file or stdin (see Stage runbooks and completion).
                              Default: unchanged.
  --clear-completion          remove the stage's completion requirement. Default: off.
  --return-stage <stageKey>   make this a judging stage: a run that completes with outcome
                              changes-needed sends the card back to this EARLIER stage with its
                              findings (at most twice before it waits for a person), and a
                              completion here must say pass or changes-needed. Default:
                              unchanged.
  --clear-return-stage        stop the stage judging: remove its return stage. Default: off.

Who may run it:
  a person: member or above in the workspace
  an agent token: the compose scope
  An agent may only send --instructions.

Calls:
  PATCH /v1/boards/:boardId/stages/:stageKey

Examples:
  supi set-stage brd_8f2c… build --instructions runbooks/build.md
  supi set-stage brd_8f2c… review --gate approval --wip 3
  supi set-stage brd_8f2c… build --clear-completion
  supi set-stage brd_8f2c… integrate --return-stage fix
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi set-stages --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi set-stages <boardId> <file|->

Replaces every stage of a board. Anything the file does not carry — another stage's
instructions, a completion rule, a concurrent edit — is discarded. To change one stage, use
set-stage.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)
  <file|->              the new pipeline: a JSON file, or - for stdin, in the same shape
                        create-board --stages takes

Who may run it:
  a person: admin or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused

Calls:
  PUT /v1/boards/:boardId/stages

Examples:
  supi board brd_8f2c… --json > p.json && $EDITOR p.json && supi set-stages brd_8f2c… p.json
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi set-stale --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi set-stale <boardId> [--hours <n>] [--off] [--on]

Sets the board's stale threshold and its switch. While on, the board's owners hear about each
stale card in their notifications at most once a day, and any push subscription to cards.stale
gets one digest per sweep that has something new to say.

Arguments:
  <boardId>             the board, as brd_… (see supi boards)

Flags:
  --hours <n>                 how long a card may wait before it counts as stale. Default:
                              unchanged (24 on a new board).
  --off                       stop this board reporting stale cards: no digest, and left out of
                              supi stale unless --hours is given
  --on                        report them again

Who may run it:
  a person: admin or above in the workspace
  an agent token: refused

Calls:
  PUT /v1/boards/:boardId/stale

Examples:
  supi set-stale brd_8f2c… --hours 48
  supi set-stale brd_8f2c… --off
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi skill --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi skill [install [--dir <dir>] ]

The agent skill for this binary, compiled into it: it always matches this version's verbs and
needs no network. With no argument it prints SKILL.md to stdout, with the version line filled
in. supi skill install writes it to <dir>/superpipeline-boards/SKILL.md — staged and renamed, so
a reader never sees half a file — replacing only that file, and prints the path. Running it
again is harmless. The installer does this for you when SKILL_DIR is set.

Arguments:
  <install>             write the skill into a skills directory instead of printing it

Flags:
  --dir <dir>                 with install: the skills directory; the file is written to
                              <dir>/superpipeline-boards/SKILL.md. Default: ~/.claude/skills.

Who may run it:
  Local: sends no credential to SuperPipeline.

Examples:
  supi skill
  supi skill install
  supi skill install --dir ~/.claude/skills --json
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi stale --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi stale [--hours <n>]

Lists the cards that are waiting and will go on waiting unless somebody acts: cards in
input-required (with the reason the card gives — blocked, repeated failure, a question, a
review, refused dispatch), and cards sitting in a stage nothing claims — no agent owns it, it
has no approval gate, and it is not the last stage. For each: the board, the card, its stage,
why it is stuck, how long it has been there, and what to do next.

Age is counted from when the card last changed state or stage. Without --hours each board's own
threshold applies and a board that switched stale cards off is left out.

Flags:
  --hours <n>                 list cards stuck at least this long, on every board, whatever its
                              switch says; 0 lists everything waiting now. Default: each board's
                              own threshold (24 unless set with set-stale).

Who may run it:
  a person: viewer or above in the workspace
  an agent token: the read scope

Calls:
  GET /v1/stale

Examples:
  supi stale
  supi stale --hours 0 --json
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi templates --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi templates

The board templates shipped with this binary: id, name, description and stage keys. Needs no
credential — they are part of the CLI, not fetched.

Who may run it:
  Local: sends no credential to SuperPipeline.

Examples:
  supi templates
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi update --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi update [--check]

Resolves the latest release tag on GitHub, downloads the asset for this platform
(supi-<platform>-<arch>; macOS and Linux only), verifies it against the release's SHA256SUMS,
and renames it over the running binary — staged in the binary's own directory so the swap is
atomic. A download that cannot be verified is refused rather than installed. A dev build is
always offered the update. No credential is involved.

Flags:
  --check                     only say whether a newer release exists; install nothing. Default:
                              off: install the newer release.

Who may run it:
  Talks to github.com only. Needs write access to the binary's directory, never sudo.

Examples:
  supi update --check
  supi update
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi version --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi version

Prints the release this binary was built as, and its platform. A binary run from a source
checkout, rather than a release, reports dev.

Who may run it:
  Local: sends no credential to SuperPipeline.

Examples:
  supi version
",
}
`;

exports[`supi help, pinned > supi whoami --help 1`] = `
{
  "exit": 0,
  "requests": [],
  "stderr": "",
  "stdout": "supi whoami

Resolves the credential exactly as every other command would and prints what it says about
itself: the principal, its kind (human or agent), the server, where the credential came from,
and when it expires. It reads the token's claims without verifying them and makes no call to
SuperPipeline, so a whoami that looks right does not prove the server will accept the token —
supi boards does. Resolving may exchange a stored device credential for a fresh token, which is
a call to the sign-in service.

An spa_ agent token is opaque and carries no principal; whoami says so rather than failing.

Who may run it:
  an agent token: any, no scope needed
  Needs a credential, but sends it only to the sign-in service, never to SuperPipeline.

Examples:
  supi whoami
  supi whoami --json
",
}
`;
